 are some things that are so thoroughly woven into our empirical web that discarding them would require overturning not just one idea, but vast swaths of physics that have been tested independently in thousands of other ways. The isotropy of the universe is one of those. If we turn out to be wrong about no centre, the error isn’t a simple oversight — it would mean our understanding of light, gravity, and thermodynamics is fundamentally broken in a way that has somehow escaped every experiment on Earth and in space. That’s possible in the ultimate philosophical sense, but it’s not a live possibility in any practical scientific sense. ### 6. And crucially, the “no centre” conclusion is not arrogant — it’s exactly the opposite. Geocentrism put us in a special place. The Copernican principle removed us from it. The modern claim that the universe has no centre is the ultimate extension of that humility: not only are we not the centre, but *no place is*. That’s a direct legacy of learning from past mistakes — it’s the anti-geocentrism. When we say “there is no centre,” we’re saying “the universe doesn’t care about a special location, and all our data screams that it has none.” It’s not a new dogma; it’s the observationally forced conclusion that came from dethroning the old one. So, while your instinct to doubt absolute certainty is healthy, in this case the certainty isn’t based on tradition — it’s based on the loud silence where a centre’s signature should be. That silence has been searched for, across the entire sky, at many wavelengths, for decades. It’s not a case of “we haven’t looked”; it’s that we looked, and the universe told us, very clearly, that there’s no centre.
